 ],
 "literal": "The ocean's roar having subsided, the seashore grove having grown dim, / the water of the shore and the dark backwater have indeed become desolate. / In the beautiful village common ground, dwelling in the palm fronds of the palmyra tree, / the ibis bird also cries gently. Today, will he / come? May you live long, friend! We, even if we fail / and feign anger, fearing separation then, / he is indeed the one who restrained this love that is difficult to separate from.",
 "translation": "The ocean's roar has subsided. The seashore grove has grown dim. The waters of the shore and the dark backwater have become desolate. Dwelling in the fronds of the palmyra tree in the village common, the ibis bird also cries out gently. Will he come today, my friend? May you live long! Even if we, failing, feign anger, he is indeed the one who has restrained this love that is so hard to part from, fearing separation.",
 "modern": "கடலின் ஒலி அடங்கி, கடற்கரைச் சோலை இருண்டு, துறையின் நீரும் கரிய கழியும் பொலிவிழந்துவிட்டன. ஊர் மன்றத்தில் உள்ள அழகிய பனைமரத்தின் மடல்களில் வாழும் அன்றில் பறவையும் மெல்லக் குரல் எழுப்புகிறது. தோழியே! நீ வாழ்க! இன்று அவர் வருவாரோ? நாம் ஊடல் கொண்டாலும், பிரிவுக்கு அஞ்சி, பிரிதற்கரிய காமத்தை அடக்கிக் கொண்டவர் அவரே அல்லவா?",
 "explanation": "The friend is speaking to the heroine as evening falls and the hero has not yet returned. She describes the coastal landscape growing quiet and desolate to mirror the heroine's anxiety. The ocean's roar has subsided, the seashore grove has darkened, and the backwaters look lackluster. The ibis bird, famous for its devotion to its mate, cries out softly from the palmyra tree, intensifying the sense of loneliness. The friend comforts the heroine by reminding her of the hero's deep love and self-restraint, noting that even if they were to feign anger, he is the one who controls his intense desire out of fear of any real separation.",
